# Little owl

The little owl is a small, flat-headed owl of open country across Europe, north
Africa and temperate Asia. Unlike most of its family it is often abroad in
daylight, perching openly on a post or a roof ridge, though the bulk of its
foraging is done at dusk and through the dark hours. Insects, earthworms and
small rodents make up most of what it takes, and it will run down prey on the
ground. It was introduced to Britain in the nineteenth century and is now
established across lowland England. See [[strigidae]].
